<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Casa de Contratación</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Casa de Contratación, or House of Trade, was the Spanish crown agency founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in 1503 to regulate trade and navigation with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. It licensed voyages, collected duties and trained pilots. Its monopoly directed American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> through Spanish ports, generating customs revenues for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/casa-de-contratacion.jpg" alt="Casa de Contratación" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FlyingCrimsonPig · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Real_Alc%C3%A1zar_de_Sevilla,_Sevilla_(42052074324).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a> created the House of Trade of the Indies eleven years after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>'s landfall in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> in 1492; its official name was La Casa y <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">Indias</a>. It was the Spanish counterpart of the Portuguese Casa da India of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. Its first officers were Sancho de Matienzo as treasurer, Jimeno de Bribiesca as contador and Francisco Pinelo as factor, and they soon controlled the economic development of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. Until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a> was founded in 1524, the Casa had broad powers over overseas affairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Unlike the later chartered East India Companies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English, the Casa was an arm of the crown. It approved every voyage of exploration and trade, licensed captains and emigrants, administered commercial law, kept secret information on routes and discoveries, and settled the estates of Spaniards who died overseas. In theory no Spaniard could sail without its approval, though smuggling was common. It levied the quinto real, a 20 per cent tax on all precious metals entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and other duties of between 10 and 40 per cent, while each ship had to carry a clerk to log its goods.</p><p>The Casa kept the Padrón Real, the official and secret map from which every Spanish ship's charts were copied, first made in 1508. Amerigo Vespucci was its first piloto mayor from 1508 until his death in 1512, training pilots in its navigation school; later cosmographers included Sebastian Cabot, Alonso de Santa Cruz and Pedro de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. Trade itself passed through the merchants' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, the Consulado de mercaderes, and the convoys of the Flota de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">Indias</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the late 17th century the Casa had fallen into bureaucratic gridlock as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> struggled to pay for both its wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and a global empire, and the riches carried from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> were often signed over to the crown's creditors before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleons</a> reached port. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> kings reduced its power and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, moving it to Cádiz in 1717. Charles III limited its powers further, and his son Charles IV abolished it in 1790, a reorganisation that also ended the treasure fleets.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Casa de Contratación” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1492 the crown claimed a monopoly of trade with the Americas, organised from 1503 through the <b>Casa de Contratación</b> in Seville, and American silver flowed into Castile, enriching the crown but also fuelling inflation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Seville held the monopoly of trade with the Americas through the <b>Casa de Contratación</b>, and convoys of the treasure fleets brought silver from the mines of Potosí in Peru, discovered in 1545, and Zacatecas in New Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Casa de Contratación</b> in Seville, founded in 1503, regulated colonial trade through a monopoly that channelled American silver through a single Castilian port.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
